data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?** is waiting on you. Its buttons are in the Asks panel.\n\n**The short version:** carts have grown past the 4 KB session cookie, so the cart has to live on the server. The cookie would then hold only an ID that points to it. The question is which store holds it.\n\n**Example:** a shopper adds three items on their phone, then opens the site on their laptop later that day.\n- **Redis (a1):** the cart is kept in memory, which is very fast. It's a good fit for something that expires, since abandoned carts can time out automatically. The risk is that Redis is designed as a cache. Unless persistence is turned on, a restart or eviction can drop carts without warning, and the shopper finds an empty cart. Running it reliably means another service to operate.\n- **Postgres (a2):** the cart is a row in the database you already run. It survives restarts and is easy to query, for example to see how many carts were abandoned or to look up a customer's cart when they call support. It's slower than Redis per request, but for cart traffic that difference is usually small. Expiry needs a scheduled cleanup job rather than a built-in timeout.\n\n**What each choice leads to:**\n- Choose **Redis** if cart reads are very frequent or you expect very high traffic, and you're willing to configure persistence and run it as a second system.\n- Choose **Postgres** if you want carts to be durable and easy to inspect, with one fewer system to run.\n\n**My recommendation:** Postgres (a2), which matches the main agent's recommendation. Losing a shopper's cart is the costly failure, and Postgres makes that very unlikely with no extra infrastructure. Redis would be worth reconsidering only if measured cart load shows the database is a bottleneck.\n\n**Limits of this explanation:** the main checkout (`project`) has no tracked files yet, and I found no cart, session, Redis or Postgres code in it. So this comparison is general, not based on the existing codebase. I didn't run the explain skill because it isn't in my skill list, so I explained it directly.\n\nTell me which option you pick, or ask about anything you want more detail on. Once you choose, I'll resolve A1 with your choice and a note for the main agent."}}

